import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Credentials Vault" activeNav="/platform/credentials-vault">
  <h1>Credentials Vault</h1>
  <p>
    The vault holds signing and publishing material for the whole workspace: keystores,
    distribution certificates, provisioning profiles, service account keys. Each credential has an
    encrypted payload of secure fields and, for the types that need one, an attached secure file.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="credentials-vault"
    alt="Credentials Vault listing eight credentials with type, secure field count, the app that uses them, and validity"
    caption="Credentials belong to the workspace and attach to build profiles."
  />

  <h2>Reading the list</h2>
  <p>
    Each row answers three questions: what it is, who uses it, and whether it is still good.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Column</th><th>What to look at</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Credential</td>
        <td>
          Name, type, how many secure fields it stores, and whether a secure file has been
          uploaded. <code>no secure file uploaded</code> on a keystore means the credential is
          incomplete and a build using it will fail. External credentials show the vault provider
          and resource path instead.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Used by</td>
        <td>
          The application and identifier whose build profile this credential is attached to. A
          credential attached to nothing is dead weight, and the <strong>Unattached</strong> filter
          finds them.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Validity</td>
        <td>
          Expiry date, or <code>no expiry</code> for material that does not expire, such as an
          Android keystore. External credentials may also show a verification status badge.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>The status filters are the useful part</h3>
  <p>
    The pills across the top are counters and filters at once, and they are how you audit a
    workspace in a few seconds:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Filter</th><th>Finds</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Expired</td><td>Material that can no longer sign anything. Builds using it fail now.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Expiring</td><td>Material inside its warning window. This is the one to check before a release.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unattached</td><td>Credentials no build profile uses — either stale, or a setup that was never finished.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No file</td><td>Credentials whose secure file is missing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No expiry</td><td>Material with no expiry date, which is normal for keystores.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Verification failed</td><td>External vault references that could not be resolved. Check the resource path and provider credentials.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A pill with a zero count is disabled rather than hidden, so the absence of expiring credentials
    is visible instead of merely implied.
  </p>

  <h3>Expanding a credential</h3>
  <Screenshot
    src="credential-detail-expanded"
    alt="Expanded credential detail showing attachments, file info, and vault verification status"
    caption="Click a credential name to see where it is attached and its full metadata."
  />
  <p>
    Click a credential name to expand its detail panel. The left column shows every build profile
    this credential is attached to, with the app name and identifier. The right column shows the
    secure file (name and size), the last update time, and a <strong>Show password</strong> button
    that decrypts the payload fields for 60 seconds after password confirmation.
  </p>

  <h2>Adding a credential</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Add credential</strong> asks for the type first, because the type decides the rest of
    the form: an Android keystore wants a store password, key alias and key password plus the
    <code>.jks</code> or <code>.p12</code> file; an iOS certificate wants a password and a
    <code>.p12</code>; a service account wants a JSON key.
  </p>
  <p>
    Secure fields are encrypted at rest and are never returned in a listing. Editing shows them as
    already-stored rather than displaying the value, and leaving a field blank keeps the current
    secret rather than clearing it.
  </p>

  <h3>Uniqueness checking</h3>
  <p>
    The workspace has a <strong>Credential uniqueness</strong> setting, on the
    <a href="/platform/team-access">Team access</a> screen. With it on, new credential forms look
    for an existing credential of the same type with the same file and identifying fields, and warn
    before you create a duplicate. Duplicated signing material is a real hazard: two keystores that
    differ only in name make it impossible to tell which one shipped a release.
  </p>

  <h2>Attaching to a build profile</h2>
  <p>
    A credential does nothing until it is attached to a build profile on an identifier. That link is
    made from the identifier — see
    <a href="/platform/applications">Applications → Identifiers</a> — and it is what decides which
    material a given build signs with.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because the vault is workspace-wide, one keystore can serve several apps. Deleting it affects
    every profile that references it, which is why deletion is refused while builds depend on it.
  </p>

  <h2>External vaults</h2>
  <Screenshot
    src="credentials-vault-external"
    alt="Credentials Vault filtered to external credentials showing vault provider and resource path"
    caption="External credentials show the provider label and resource path instead of file info."
  />
  <p>
    A credential can point at material held in an external secret manager instead of storing it in
    App Manager. The form then collects a <strong>provider</strong> and a <strong>resource</strong>
    reference rather than the secret itself, and your CI/CD pipeline resolves it at build time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the <strong>Storage</strong> filter and select <strong>In my vault</strong> to see only
    external credentials. Each row shows the vault provider (e.g. AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp
    Vault) and the resource path in monospace, instead of the file name and encrypted field count
    that local credentials display.
  </p>

  <h3>Supported providers</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Resource format</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>AWS Secrets Manager</td><td>ARN, e.g. <code>arn:aws:secretsmanager:eu-central-1:123456789012:secret:name</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>HashiCorp Vault</td><td>Secret path, e.g. <code>secret/data/mobile/ios-distribution</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Azure Key Vault</td><td>Secret URL, e.g. <code>https://my-vault.vault.azure.net/secrets/name</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Google Secret Manager</td><td>Resource name, e.g. <code>projects/my-project/secrets/name/versions/latest</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>fastlane match</td><td>Git URL with context, e.g. <code>git@github.com:acme/certs.git#appstore/com.acme.app</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Custom</td><td>Any address your pipeline understands</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Vault metadata</h3>
  <p>
    Each external reference carries optional key/value metadata — for example
    <code>region: eu-central-1</code> or <code>namespace: ci</code>. This metadata is stored in
    App Manager and passed to your pipeline so it knows how to resolve the resource. App Manager
    never reads the secret itself; it only stores the reference.
  </p>

  <h3>Verification</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>App Manager does not check the reference itself.</strong> It holds no credentials for
    your vault and no network path to it, so it cannot. Verification is a verdict <em>reported to</em>
    it by something that can reach the vault — your build — and the badge in the validity column
    shows the last verdict received:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><em>No badge</em></td><td>A build last reported that the reference resolved.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Not verified</td><td>Nothing has reported on it yet. This is where every external credential starts.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Verification failed</td><td>A build reported that it could not resolve the reference. Expanding the credential shows the reason it gave.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Nothing here runs on a schedule, and no verdict expires: a reference verified six months ago
    still shows as verified. The badge tells you what a build last saw, not what is true right now,
    so a release preflight should re-report rather than trust it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reporting is done from the pipeline with
    <a href="/cli/credentials"><code>appmctl credentials verify</code></a>, which needs the
    <code>credentials:verify</code> scope — separate from <code>reveal</code>, since saying whether a
    reference resolves is not reading the secret behind it:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    code={`appmctl credentials verify "$CREDENTIAL_ID" --status verified --metadata '{"region":"eu-central-1"}'
appmctl credentials verify "$CREDENTIAL_ID" --status failed  --error 'secret not found'`}
  />
  <p>
    The first transition into failed also raises the
    <code>credential.verification_failed</code> event, so a broken reference can reach
    <a href="/platform/integrations/notifications">a chat channel</a> or
    <a href="/platform/integrations/webhooks">a webhook</a> the moment a build trips over it. A build
    retrying in a loop does not re-notify.
  </p>
  <p>
    A build can also report an expiry it read out of the vault, which lands in the validity column
    like any other expiry date — that is how an externally held certificate shows up in the
    <strong>Expiring</strong> filter at all.
  </p>

  <h3>Storage mode is immutable</h3>
  <p>
    Once a credential is created, its storage mode cannot be changed. To move a credential from
    local to external (or vice versa), create a new credential with the desired mode and re-attach
    it to the relevant build profiles.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which storage modes are available depends on the instance configuration and the workspace's
    <strong>Permitted credential storage</strong> setting on the
    <a href="/platform/team-access">Team access</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>Doing the same from the CLI</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/cli/credentials"><code>appmctl credentials</code></a> covers listing, creating,
    rotating payloads, replacing files, and downloading material. Two notes carry over:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Secret payloads go through <code>--data-file</code>, never an inline argument, because process
      arguments are visible to other users and land in CI logs.
    </li>
    <li>
      Downloading material needs the <code>credentials:reveal</code> scope on top of read access —
      reading a keystore is a different act from listing that one exists.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
